<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colline Metallifere</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Colline Metallifere ('metal-bearing hills') are a range of hills in southern Tuscany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, spanning the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grosseto">Grosseto</a>, Siena, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a>. Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, iron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and lead ores were worked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> and enriched cities such as Vetulonia and Populonia, and mining continued through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> into the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colline-metallifere.jpg" alt="Colline Metallifere" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LepoRello · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colline_Metallifere_view_from_Campiglia_Marittima_2012-08-27.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
